  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild and quiet weather expected through Wednesday, though\n   morning low clouds or valley fog is possible.\n\n-  Two weak systems bring showers Thursday and Friday with light\n   dusting of snow above 5,000 feet, and westerly breezes. \n\n-  Mild and dry conditions return for the weekend, with morning\n   fog remaining possible. \n\nHigh pressure building over the region will lead to stagnant \nconditions and trapped surface moisture, creating a favorable \nsetup for locally dense valley fog Tuesday morning, and again on \nWednesday morning. Motorists should exercise caution, as \nvisibility will vary significantly over short distances on local \nroadways. Fog will generally dissipate by each afternoon, allowing\ndaytime temperatures to warm near or slightly above seasonal \naverages, peaking in the 40s to low 50s.\n\nA pair of weak weather systems will pass through the region on \nThursday and Friday, bringing a return to active weather but \nminimal impacts. Light showers will primarily be focused across \nnorthwest Montana, though overall precipitation amounts will \nremain limited. Snow levels will hover near 5,000 feet. In \naddition to the showers, expect an uptick in afternoon breezes, \nparticularly on Friday.\n\nHigh pressure returns this weekend, bringing a stretch of mostly \ndry conditions, mild afternoons, and localized morning valley fog.\nBy Sunday and Monday, remnant moisture from an atmospheric river \nimpacting the Pacific Northwest coast will spill inland, bringing \nrenewed chances for light showers to northwest Montana. Looking \nfurther ahead to Nov 11-14 suggests a broader pattern shift. \nAtmospheric rivers initially aimed at the Pacific Northwest coast \nare forecast to sag southward and push inland. This transition \nwould increase the likelihood of more widespread precipitation \nacross our region, including the potential for accumulating \nmountain snowfall. Confidence in this pattern setup is low to \nmoderate, but it is certainly something we will keep an eye on.",
